@startuml state-job
!include elvira.iuml
' =============================================================================
' What: a job is declarative: a client states what it wants (spec + desired
'       state), the reconciler moves the job through these states until the
'       observed state matches; a client never calls "retry" or "revoke", it
'       applies the spec again (a new generation) or sets desired = cancelled.
' Kind: UML state machine diagram, proposal (IP-031 rev. 2). Scope: the Job
'       aggregate (one per kind + subject). The Celery task executions behind it
'       are runs (state-job-run). Omitted: the API shape, persistence.
' Source: EvilFlowersCatalog IP-031 rev. 2 (2026-11-14); apps/tasks/domain.
' =============================================================================

title Job state machine: desired vs observed, driven by the reconciler (proposal, 2026-11-14)

state "waiting" as waiting {
  state "pending" as pending : generation > observed_generation\nor a retry is due
  state "blocked" as blocked : definition.check() refused\n(LCP off, active loans …);\nre-checked on every reconcile
}

state "active" as active {
  state "queued" as queued : a run was dispatched
  state "running" as running : the run started
}

state "cancelling" as cancelling : desired = cancelled,\nrun still active

state "settled" as settled {
  state "succeeded" as succeeded : the current generation's\nrun succeeded
  state "failed" as failed : no attempts left
  state "cancelled" as cancelled : nothing runs
}

[*] -[$accent()]down-> pending : <b>apply(kind, subject, spec)</b>\n<b>generation = 1</b>

pending -right-> blocked : reconcile:\ncheck refused
pending -[$accent()]down-> queued : <b>reconcile: check ok →</b>\n<b>dispatch(run)</b>
blocked -down-> queued : reconcile:\ncheck ok → dispatch
queued -[$accent()]down-> running : <b>run started</b>
running -[$accent()]down-> succeeded : <b>run succeeded</b>\n<b>(+ report applied)</b>
active -up-> pending : run failed,\nattempts left
active -down-> failed : run failed / lost /\nrejected, none left
running -right-> cancelling : desired = cancelled
cancelling -down-> cancelled : run ended (outcome\ndiscarded) or terminated
queued -down-> cancelled : desired = cancelled\n(revoked, never starts)
waiting -down-> cancelled : desired = cancelled
failed -right-> succeeded : observe():\ndomain done
settled -up-> pending : apply(spec)\ngeneration + 1
[*] -down-> settled : discovered by\ndefinition.observe()

legend bottom left
  |= Notation |= Meaning |
  | box | job state (observed); its text: what holds in that state |
  | outer box | composite: an arrow from it leaves any state inside |
  | arrow label | trigger → effect; **apply(spec)** and **desired** are the only client inputs |
  | blue arrows | the normal path of one generation |
endlegend

caption Clients write only spec and desired state (POST /api/v1/jobs applies, PATCH changes); the reconciler and the run events write the state.\nRetry = apply the spec again (generation + 1); revoke = desired cancelled; re-encrypt = apply {reencrypt: true}. A refused check is a state\n(blocked), re-checked by the reconciler, so a job waiting for LCP or for loans to end proceeds by itself. Apps that own domain state (LCP,\nextraction) keep their jobs in step through definition.observe(), so every domain job is listed even without a client's intent.
footer ELVIRA · FIIT STU · EvilFlowersCatalog · docs/diagrams/state-job.puml
@enduml
